now past the first hurdle for getting the job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.040
This is actually a special election&nbsp;
that's going on in Alaska since Don Young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.080
died earlier this year after representing&nbsp;
the at large district in Alaska for 49 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.840
Now, Alaska has this interesting system where they&nbsp;
have all of their candidates, regardless of party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.200
appear on the same ballot for a first round of&nbsp;
voting. The top four vote getters then move on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.960
to the next round of voting, where ranked choice&nbsp;
voting is used to determine who comes out on top.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.760
We just had the first round of voting and we're&nbsp;
still getting the votes in. But as of now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.440
the results show that Palin has 29.8% of the&nbsp;
vote, followed by another Republican named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:01:00.400
Nick Begich at 19.3%. Then we have Al GROSS at&nbsp;
12.5% and Democrat Mary Pallotta at 7.5%. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.080
those should be the four who advance and move on&nbsp;
to the second round. Notably, not making the cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:12.880
yet still getting an impressive 4.5% of the vote&nbsp;
was Democratic Socialist candidate Santa Claus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.960
which I didn't realize that Santa lived up&nbsp;
in Alaska. I thought he lived up in Canada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.440
or like maybe the international territory that&nbsp;
makes up the North Pole. But apparently he's in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.840
Alaska. RESIDENT And who knows, maybe he'll get&nbsp;
them next time. Palin's near 30% in the vote is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.320
a commanding lead ahead of everyone else. But then&nbsp;
when you note that there's something like 50 other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:38.800
candidates, you have to think about how the other&nbsp;
voters are going to coalesce when the candidate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.840
number drops down to four. Also, you have ranked&nbsp;
choice voting, so that makes it a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.560
harder to predict compared to a normal election.&nbsp;
So who knows what will happen in the second round.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.640
